   "text": "(2020)."
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 182.4,
   "w": 102.8,
   "size": 15.9,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "Observations"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 221.5,
   "w": 644.4,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "The literary symmetries invite the reader to compare the corresponding sections. The “image of God”"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 242.4,
   "w": 664.2,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "statements are immediately followed by sections that unpack the meaning and significance of the divine"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 262.3,
   "w": 43.2,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "image."
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 301.1,
   "w": 195.1,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "1. “And Let Them Rule Over …”"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 339.0,
   "w": 686.2,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "In Hebrew grammar and syntax, when you have a verb of command or invitation (called a “jussive”) followed"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 359.4,
   "w": 628.0,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "by a clause that also has a jussive (“and let them rule …”), it is meant to be understood as a purpose"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 379.7,
   "w": 489.2,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "statement: “Let us make human in our image … in order that they may rule …”"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 413.7,
   "w": 645.1,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "For this point, see Thomas Lambdin’s book Introduction to Biblical Hebrew (1971 edition), section 107c."
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 452.5,
   "w": 245.2,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "2. “Male and Female” = “In His Image”"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 491.4,
   "w": 527.8,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "Notice in Genesis 1:27 how the three-line poem has three key elements in each line."
  },
  {
   "x": 67.6,
   "y": 541.8,
   "w": 172.0,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "Parallelism of Genesis 1:27"
  },
  {
   "x": 67.6,
   "y": 590.4,
   "w": 35.6,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "Line 1"
  },
  {
   "x": 179.6,
   "y": 590.9,
   "w": 79.6,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "God created"
  },
  {
   "x": 363.0,
   "y": 590.9,
   "w": 44.0,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "human"
  },
  {
   "x": 484.1,
   "y": 590.9,
   "w": 76.3,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "in his image"
  },
  {
   "x": 67.6,
   "y": 634.0,
   "w": 37.7,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "Line 2"
  },
  {
   "x": 179.6,
   "y": 634.5,
   "w": 70.1,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "He created"
  },
  {
   "x": 363.0,
   "y": 634.5,
   "w": 24.0,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "him"
  },
  {
   "x": 484.1,
   "y": 634.5,
   "w": 126.1,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "in the image of God"
  },
  {
   "x": 67.6,
   "y": 677.7,
   "w": 38.4,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "Line 3"
  },
  {
   "x": 179.6,
   "y": 678.2,
   "w": 70.1,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "He created"
  },
  {
   "x": 363.0,
   "y": 678.2,
   "w": 33.0,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "them"
  },
  {
   "x": 484.1,
   "y": 678.2,
   "w": 105.1,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "male and female"
  },
  {
   "x": 67.6,
   "y": 722.4,
   "w": 656.8,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "Parallelism of Genesis 1:27. Created by Tim Mackie for BibleProject Classroom: Heaven and Earth (2019)."
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 769.4,
   "w": 684.9,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "This poetic parallelism makes clear that the divine image is constituted in some crucial way by the duality of"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 789.3,
   "w": 689.9,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "gender. Somehow God is imaged in a special way through the unity of humanity as one species that consists"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 809.7,
   "w": 662.5,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "of two distinct others. Humanity is one and more than one at the same time, and this offers an important"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 830.1,
   "w": 192.2,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "image of God’s divine identity."
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 868.9,
   "w": 146.8,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "3. The Preposition “In”"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 904.6,
   "w": 688.8,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "The preposition “in” (Heb. be-/-ב) attached to “his image” means “in the place of” or “instead of” (called beth"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 927.1,
   "w": 698.0,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "pretii, see Gesenius’ Hebrew Grammar, sec. 102p). Therefore, this verse can be read as “God created human in"
  },
  {
   "x": 46.6,
   "y": 947.5,
   "w": 231.7,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "the place of his image/as his image.”"
  },
  {
   "x": 133.9,
   "y": 50.3,
   "w": 147.0,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "over the fish of the sea"
  },
  {
   "x": 133.9,
   "y": 70.7,
   "w": 183.4,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "and over the birds of the sky"
  },
  {
   "x": 133.9,
   "y": 91.1,
   "w": 290.8,
   "size": 13.6,
   "flags": 0,
   "text": "and over every living creature that creeps on"
  },
